ear-fatal overdose were both on board  with this highly public airing of their  family trauma i just think that with  everything that happened to me and to  william something good has to come from  that brutal total honesty is the best  medicine  i've been told and you know keeping  secrets make you sick but  turning toward your painful story and  owning it  is essential or else it owns you he kept  daily going back to substances to alter  his feeling mcgee has now dug in  speaking to any students who will listen  and delivering a wake-up call to parents  about their kids loss of hope do you  feel kids today are nose to nose with  despair in a way that maybe their  parents generation wasn't  absolutely something has changed  generationally i consistently hear from  them  i'm not sure if i can get a job as good  as my dad had i'm not sure i could buy  the house we have i'm not sure i'll be  able to buy a car then add in fallout  from the pandemic a study on its effect  by duke university psychologist kenneth  dodge shows the greatest spike in  substance use was seen in adults 19 to  22  and the children of parents with  substance issues run an even higher risk  for substance abuse a young adult looks  to her or his  parents about what their hopes were  and if that person sees difficulty that  makes it harder to feel hope a fact that  still cripples david mcgee our children  were falling apart before our eyes but i  was so lost in our own my struggle that  i couldn't see it so to help a kid who's  in crisis  before you look just at the kid  spend some time at the mirror and you  will not always like what you see hello  his family has started the william mcgee  center to address substance abuse at ole  miss  and then share what they learn with  other schools we have to get better at  this as a country  david mcgee's contribution is the raw  story of his family's heartbreak and  healing and he's determined to tell it  to give others hope  but you can  wrestle  meaning even from  the deepest darkest pain the darkest  moment  can  you know often be a real energy source  for  doing good and doing well and living  well  what a brave thing john talk about  cutting yourself open and exposing  yourself warts and all that's very  powerful yeah you know he said look if  you're going to do this  you got to go wide open you got to go to  the bone or else it's not going to be  helpful to other families that are  reading the story and he wants to put  that pain into service of broadening out  to address the generation a generation  of 20-somethings teens that grew up on  active shooter drills grew up on climate  crisis grew up on a political system  that seems incapable of addressing their  needs  if  if substance abuse is in an attempt to  medicate that pain understand this  generation's pain yeah it's different  it is different it is different i i do  think parents should hold themselves  accountable and ask themselves what they  did to contribute to a situation but  david seems to put a lot of blame on  himself looking in the mirror i mean  he's processing not just the death of  his son but the near death of hudson who  we just saw from an overdose so i think  his look inward is  he's carrying a weight that's a lot  heavier than even other people who've  dealt with stuff like that and i think  when people deal with tragedy the  easiest thing maybe the first thing you  want to do is ask why you look up to the  heavens and you say why but you rarely  look in the mirror and ask why and  that's what i don't think that's the  whole point but i don't know there's any  rational explanation for this sort of  stuff i mean my dad was a hard drug user  for most of his life i'm not there  i think you're raising an important  important point tony there is a gener  genetic component to some of this stuff  but it's not just that there's a whole  uh  another dimension of assessing pain  right  i wonder what's a difficult decision for  the family because listen it's extremely  personal you're talking about family and  his marital infidelity which is very  difficult i would imagine from his  wife's point of view going through what  they were going through with their  children i wonder was it hard for them  to say we're going to expose everybody i  love when he said we're nobody yet we're  just everybody everybody that is unsung  hero of the whole thing  kent mcgee that the wife was after  spending the day with the family there  is such strength and grace there it was  a privilege to be in their presence yeah  was that a little grandchild running  yeah they lived thinking right in the  backyard continuous houses so nice  building they're absolutely our  rebuilding it's really nice that was a  very powerful story thank you jim  axelrod

